            <ArticleTitle>Neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipocalin after off pump versus on pump coronary artery surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>22-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/1354750X.2013.863974</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Cardiac surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare plasma and urinary neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipocalin (P-/U-NGAL) in on-pump (n = 43) versus off-pump (n = 40) surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We obtained perioperative P-/U-NGAL and outcome data.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">P-/U-NGAL increased after surgery. P-NGAL was higher post-surgery in on pump patients (139 versus 67 µg L(-1); p &lt; 0.001), but not at 24 h. There were no differences in U-NGAL. Correlation between P-/U-NGAL and plasma creatinine was weak.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">P-NGAL acts like a neutrophil activation biomarker and U-NGAL like a tubular injury marker.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">On-pump patients had greater neutrophil activation. On- versus off-pump surgery had similar impact on tubular cells.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
